@prefix : <https://w3id.org/ddta/process#> .
@prefix owl: <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix ddta: <https://w3id.org/ddta/core#> .
@base <https://w3id.org/ddta/process> .

<https://w3id.org/ddta/process>
    a owl:Ontology ;
    rdfs:label "DDTA Process Ontology (experimental bootstrap)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Experimental DDTA module for domain-independent process composition and control flow. This experimental module adds workflow-contextual process placements and identifiable directed control-flow transitions. Guards, events, concurrency, executions and other dynamic semantics remain deferred. DDTA Core terms keep their original identities and meanings."@en .

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# FIRST APPROVED EXPERIMENTAL TERMS: contextual use of reusable processes
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

:ProcessStep a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Process step"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "An identifiable planned placement of a reusable process within a particular workflow. A step is not the referenced process definition or an individual execution. Being a part of a workflow does not determine execution order or imply successful performance."@en .

:referencesProcess a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain :ProcessStep ;
    rdfs:range ddta:Process ;
    rdfs:label "references process"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Relates a workflow-specific process step to the reusable process definition assigned to that step. This relation does not imply invocation or execution, ordering, dependency, process containment, or exclusive use within one workflow."@en .

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SECOND APPROVED EXPERIMENTAL TERMS: flow nodes and first-class transitions
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

:FlowNode a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Flow node"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "A position in a planned process control-flow graph that may be an endpoint of directed transitions. This abstraction does not imply execution, temporal occurrence, or any branch/fork/join semantics by itself."@en .

:ProcessStep rdfs:subClassOf :FlowNode .

:ControlFlowTransition a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Control-flow transition"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "An identifiable, directed, planned control-flow connection between flow nodes in a process context. It specifies a possible control path, not an observed movement, a data transfer, an intrinsic dependency between reusable process definitions, or proof of execution. Conditions, triggering and concurrency semantics require separate explicit modeling."@en .

:fromNode a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain :ControlFlowTransition ;
    rdfs:range :FlowNode ;
    rdfs:label "from node"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Identifies the source node of a planned control-flow transition. A transition's membership in the same workflow as both endpoints must be validated separately; OWL domain/range are not integrity constraints."@en .

:toNode a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain :ControlFlowTransition ;
    rdfs:range :FlowNode ;
    rdfs:label "to node"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Identifies the destination node of a planned control-flow transition. Neither completion, eligibility, nor immediate runtime execution is implied; endpoint multiplicity must be validated outside OWL when required."@en .

# ============================================================================
# STATUS: EXPERIMENTAL_NON_CANONICAL / SECOND FOCUSED EXTENSION
# ============================================================================
# Ontology IRI:     https://w3id.org/ddta/process
# Term namespace:   https://w3id.org/ddta/process#
# Source file:      projects/ddta-semantic-workspace/ontology/process.ttl
#
# The w3id.org/ddta path has NOT been registered/verified for public resolution.
# No owl:imports yet: ontology resolution/loading must be tested locally before
# committing to import semantics. Core remains the source of truth for
# ddta:Function, ddta:Process, ddta:hasInput, ddta:hasOutput, ddta:hasPart,
# ddta:realizesFunction and all other existing generic terms.
#
# The implementation backlog below is COMMENTS, not declarations.
# The approved test vocabulary comprises ProcessStep, referencesProcess,
# FlowNode, ControlFlowTransition, fromNode and toNode.
# Future candidate names do not authorize their use in project documents.
#
# ============================================================================
# PROCESS DESIGN INVARIANTS (TO BE TESTED AS TERMS ARE ADDED)
# ============================================================================
# 1. Function = stable capability; Process = structured pattern of activities;
#    ProcessStep = a planned, contextual placement of an activity within a
#    particular process. Do not confuse a step with the function it uses.
# 2. Planned process structure != actual process execution or its outcome.
# 3. Shared output/input does NOT, by itself, entail execution order.
# 4. Composition (ddta:hasPart) does NOT, by itself, entail order or control.
# 5. A documentation Decision (DDTA authoring construct) is NOT a runtime branch.
# 6. A Condition describes an applicability rule; its truth in a specific run
#    requires explicit contextual facts/evaluation, not missing RDF triples.
# 7. No domain-specific AI, deployment or STRIDE concepts belong here.
# 8. RDF semantics, SPARQL selection and Mermaid notation remain separate.
#
# ============================================================================
# CANDIDATE IMPLEMENTATION BACKLOG -- NOT YET APPROVED
# ============================================================================
#
# [IMPLEMENTED A] ProcessStep and referencesProcess
#   A step is a workflow-specific placement, NOT a ddta:Process subclass.
#   ddta:hasPart (workflow -> step) provides composition context.
#   referencesProcess (step -> ddta:Process) preserves process identity.
#   No implied invocation, execution, ordering or temporal dependency.
#   Tested on DEC-C6-01 and a clearly hypothetical reuse scenario.
#   Function-to-step mapping remains deferred. Inspired by P-Plan/OWL-S.
#
# [IMPLEMENTED B] Explicit directed control-flow transitions
#   FlowNode generalizes ProcessStep for possible future gateways.
#   ControlFlowTransition has fromNode/toNode and is a part of its workflow.
#   This is NOT a global 'precedes' predicate on reusable processes.
#   A transition is a planned route, not an execution/provenance record.
#   Test: DEC-C6-01 six placements, five transitions in a linear path.
#   Endpoint membership, cardinality and linear topology are checked by a
#   focused validator rather than assumed from OWL domain/range.
#   Process modeling references: BPMN sequence flows, OWL-S control flow.
#
# [CANDIDATE C] Branches and guards
#   Possible concepts: Condition, Branch, conditional transition/guard.
#   Distinguish event 'image received' from condition 'image available'.
#   Consider mutually exclusive vs inclusive branch and default branch.
#   First possible test case: image-present and image-absent triage paths
#   at Decision/FR level, NOT as a new MR-level architectural fact.
#
# [DEFERRED] Concurrency, parallelism, fork/join and synchronization
#   Do not confuse concurrency (overlap/interleaving possible) with actual
#   simultaneous execution. Specify start/completion semantics when needed.
#
# [DEFERRED] Loop, retry, timeout, failure and compensation paths
#   Define only for a documented process that requires them.
#
# [DEFERRED] Execution/provenance
#   Planned steps, executed activities, observations and outcomes are distinct.
#   Compare PROV-O / P-Plan before introducing execution-specific vocabulary.
#
# [DEFERRED] Lifecycle/event/time context
#   A relation may be qualified by time interval, lifecycle phase or events;
#   these differ from runtime guards on a process branch.
#   Coordinate with a potential ddta-lifecycle module and OWL-Time/PROV-O;
#   do not duplicate event identities or make 'valid from/to' part of every
#   process assertion by default.
#
# ============================================================================
# INCREMENTAL ADOPTION RULE
# ============================================================================
# For EVERY new concept/property, in a separate reviewed change:
#   1. Identify one source-supported Decision/FR example and semantic gap.
#   2. State exact meaning, intended scope, domain/range and non-inferences.
#   3. Compare reuse/alignment with P-Plan, OWL-S, BPMN and (if relevant)
#      PROV-O / OWL-Time. Palantir is a conceptual reference, not a source
#      of RDF/OWL axioms to copy.
#   4. Add minimal Turtle vocabulary + positive/negative example + tests.
#   5. Verify RDF parse and intended SPARQL results; use SHACL if needed.
#   6. Only then adapt visualization YAML and generate/check Mermaid.
#   7. Reassess integration with Core without editing Core automatically.
#
# First test case: DEC-C6-01 contextual placements (prior increment).
# Second test case: five workflow-local transitions joining six placements.
# Next: joint review of definitions and ontology module catalog proposal.
# Later: conditional branches from a documented triage Decision/FR.
